>
  <h1>Permission-aware AI incident investigation</h1>
  <p class="dateline">Christopher Uzochukwu · 26 October 2026 · Africa/Lagos</p>

  <h2>Problem</h2>
  <p>
    At Mews, one of the harder operational problems was investigating failures across hotel-system
    integrations. A single customer report could require an engineer to search support tickets,
    application logs, traces, API responses and database records before reconstructing what actually
    happened. The initial symptom was often misleading: what looked like an application bug could be
    an upstream API change, stale credentials, malformed customer data, an out-of-order event or a
    partial failure during a retry. Complex investigations could take up to three days because the
    evidence was fragmented across different systems.
  </p>

  <h2>Constraints</h2>
  <p>
    The main constraints were security, incomplete evidence and the unreliability of probabilistic
    output. Engineers had different access permissions, and the workflow could not expose data simply
    because the model requested it. External systems could also return incomplete or contradictory
    information. I therefore enforced permissions before retrieval, recorded every investigation step,
    categorized tool failures separately from product failures, and made uncertainty explicit. The
    system never automatically changed production data or closed an incident; an engineer remained
    responsible for verifying the evidence and deciding what action to take.
  </p>

  <h2>Architecture</h2>
  <p>
    Deterministic code handled retrieval, filtering, correlation and timeline construction. An LLM
    was then used to summarize the evidence, identify likely failure categories and suggest the next
    diagnostic step. Its output followed a defined structure and included the evidence behind each
    conclusion rather than presenting an unsupported answer.
  </p>

  <h2>Contribution</h2>
  <p>
    I built a Python-based investigation workflow that collected the relevant evidence and organized
    it into a single, traceable investigation. I chose a narrower workflow in which deterministic
    tools gathered evidence and the model assisted with synthesis.
  </p>

  <h2>Trade-offs</h2>
  <p>
    The central trade-off was speed versus confidence. Giving the model more autonomy could have
    produced faster-looking answers, but it also increased the risk of a confident diagnosis based on
    missing context. I chose a narrower workflow in which deterministic tools gathered evidence and
    the model assisted with synthesis. That made the system less impressive as an autonomous agent,
    but considerably safer and more useful during real incidents.
  </p>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    For representative complex cases, it reduced investigation time from as much as three days to
    approximately one hour.
  </p>

  <h2>What I would improve</h2>
  <p>
    If I rebuilt it today, I would create a versioned evaluation dataset from previously resolved
    incidents and measure diagnostic accuracy, evidence coverage, latency and cost before every
    release. I would also add stronger per-tool authorization, automated redaction, replayable
    investigation traces and a feedback mechanism that distinguishes an incorrect diagnosis from
    missing evidence. Finally, I would integrate approved runbooks through retrieval, while keeping
    production changes behind explicit human confirmation.
  </p>
